WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.060
MIKE WEST &gt;&gt; Hello, Internet!

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.180
Welcome to Web Accessibility.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.530
Over the next few minutes, I'd like to explain
why form elements require labels.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.029
Let's dive right in.

00:00:13.029 --> 00:00:17.190
If I give you an arbitrary `input` element
of type `text`, you don't actually have enough

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.600
information to determine what you should start
typing.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.220
Even if you know that the form in which the
element lives is requesting a mailing address,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.039
it's difficult to guess as to whether a specific
field is requesting your name, your street

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:32.829
address, your zip code, or something else
entirely.

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:34.760
You need some additional context.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:40.280
Visually, we solve this problem via layout,
and by positioning textual labels next to

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.350
the form fields.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.140
I _see_ "name" next to the field, so I know
I should type my name.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.999
Users who can't see the label, however, are
apt to be confused.

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:53.820
Let's take a look at a naïve implementation
of this labeling and determine how we can

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.820
improve it.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.969
What we have here is a name (label), and an
input field.

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.680
If we run this through a screen reader, let's
see how it sounds.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.140
CHROMEVOX &gt;&gt; Edit text.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.300
MIKE WEST &gt;&gt; "Edit text."

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.049
That doesn't actually give me any context.

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:09.820
It doesn't give me information that I would
need in order to figure out what it is that

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.659
I should start typing here.

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:15.200
We have, however, this label, and it would
be great if we could somehow semantically

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.310
bind this text to the field.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.299
In fact, HTML allows us to do exactly that
with the `label` element.

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:25.920
The first thing we're going to need to do
is uniquely identify this input field so that

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.560
we can say "Label X is associated with input
field Y."

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.259
We do that with an ID.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.090
Great.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.200
So now the input field has an ID, and we can
use that ID to label it.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.490
We'll use the `label` element, and set a `for`
attribute that contains the same value as

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.159
the ID that we've just set.

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:49.409
Alright.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.869
Let's run this through a screen reader, and
see how it sounds.

00:01:51.869 --> 00:01:53.390
CHROMEVOX &gt;&gt; [Beep] Name.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.390
Edit text.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.390
MIKE WEST &gt;&gt; "Name.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.390
Edit text."

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:57.390
That's excellent!

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.259
That gives us the context that we need in
order to start doing something useful with

00:02:00.259 --> 00:02:01.259
this form field.

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:05.531
If we want to give them even more information,
for example, a, an example of text that I

00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:10.050
should type here, we can use the `placeholder`
attribute, which is gaining support.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.860
It's part of HTML5, it's not yet supported
by all screen readers, but most assistive

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.480
technology will pick it up at some point in
the near future.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.470
Setting a `placeholder` [attribute] gives
me some visual feedback that there's something

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:24.510
interesting about this field.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.470
It gives me a hint as to what it is that I
should start typing.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.450
A screen reader like ChromeVox understands
this, and will read it out to a user.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.700
Let's see how that sounds.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.040
CHROMEVOX &gt;&gt; [Beep] Name.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.040
With hint.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.180
First and last name.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.180
Edit text.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.180
MIKE WEST &gt;&gt; "Name.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.180
With hint.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.180
First and last name.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.180
Edit text."

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.061
So it tells me that I'm in an edit field,
that it has a label, of "Name" and that I

00:02:43.061 --> 00:02:45.820
should type both my first and last name.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.950
This is perfect.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.920
For further discussion of accessibility on
the web, come visit us, at W one five Y dot

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:53.920
com.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.630
That's W fifteen Y dot com.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:56.740
Thanks for your time.

